    $astroISO_master$> Every other object in this book belongs to the Sun. It condensed from the same disk, it orbits on a closed ellipse, and if you wait long enough it comes back. The objects in this chapter belong to someone else. They were built around other stars, in other disks, out of ices that never touched our nebula, and they are passing through on orbits that do not close — arriving from the galaxy, bending once around the Sun, and leaving forever. For the whole history of astronomy this population was a certainty on paper and a blank in the catalogue: planetary systems must throw material away, so the space between the stars must be seeded with their leftovers, and yet not one such object had ever been seen. Then, in a span of eight years, three of them were caught in the act. What they teach is not one lesson but two, braided together. The first is planetary science: these are the only pieces of other solar systems we will ever hold in a telescope's gaze, and their sheer number measures a quantity we could not otherwise reach — how efficiently a forming planetary system flings its building blocks into the dark. The second is a lesson in method, forced on us by the first one found, which was strange enough that a serious voice proposed it was a piece of alien technology. Learning to handle that claim correctly — to be genuinely open to it and genuinely disciplined about it at once — is as much a part of this chapter as the orbits and the ices.

## A class of object younger than the smartphone

Almost everything in the solar system was known to the ancients, to Galileo, or to the great surveys of the nineteenth and twentieth centuries. Interstellar objects are different. The category is younger than the phone in your pocket. Before **19 October 2017** it had exactly zero members. It is one of the very few times in modern astronomy that an entire class of solar-system body has gone from hypothetical to observed inside a single human career — and then, having gone from zero to one, went on to three inside a decade, which is itself a data point about how common they must be.

The naming convention was invented on the spot and tells the story cleanly. The prefix is a running count, the letter **I** stands for *interstellar*, and the name follows:

- **1I/'Oumuamua** — the first, 2017, the anomaly.
- **2I/Borisov** — the second, 2019, the reassuringly ordinary comet.
- **3I/ATLAS** — the third, 2025, the ancient one.

Three objects is not a population; it is barely a rumour of one. But each was informative out of all proportion to its size, and the three together already sketch the shape of the thing. To read them you first need to know what makes an orbit *interstellar* in the first place — because that, and not composition or appearance, is what admits an object to this club.

{{image: Interstellar object | An artist's impression of an interstellar object passing through the inner solar system. Unlike every comet and asteroid native to the Sun, these bodies are not gravitationally bound: they arrive from the galaxy on open, hyperbolic paths and leave on them, never to return.}}

## The signature is the orbit, and the orbit does not close

Everything the Sun owns moves on a **closed** path: a circle or an ellipse, endlessly repeating, with eccentricity $e$ somewhere between 0 and just under 1. The energy of such an orbit is negative — the object is bound, trapped in the Sun's gravitational well, and no matter how far out it wanders it eventually falls back. A comet from the Oort cloud on a million-year orbit is still bound; its eccentricity may be 0.9999, but it is less than one, and it will return.

An object with eccentricity **greater than one** is on a **hyperbola**, and a hyperbola is an open curve. The Sun bends its path but cannot hold it. The object comes in from infinity with energy to spare, whips around perihelion, and climbs back out to infinity, slowing but never stopping, retaining forever a residual speed. That residual is the tell. Write the vis-viva equation, which relates speed $v$ to distance $r$ and semi-major axis $a$:

$$v^2 = GM_\odot\left(\frac{2}{r} - \frac{1}{a}\right)$$

For a bound ellipse $a>0$, and as $r\to\infty$ the speed goes to zero — the object is just barely tethered. For a hyperbola $a$ is **negative**, and as $r\to\infty$ the bracket does not vanish; it leaves behind a finite speed called the **hyperbolic excess velocity**:

$$v_\infty = \sqrt{\frac{GM_\odot}{|a|}}$$

This $v_\infty$ is the speed the object had relative to the Sun *before it ever felt the Sun* — its cruising speed through interstellar space, minus the boost gravity lends and then reclaims. And it is not a small number. The stars in the Sun's neighbourhood drift relative to one another at typically **20 to 60 kilometres per second**. Anything sharing that motion, wandering free of any star, will meet the Sun at a comparable speed — far too fast to capture. A bound comet crawls into the inner system; an interstellar object *tears* through it.

So the entry ticket to this chapter is a single measured quantity: an eccentricity comfortably above one, or equivalently a $v_\infty$ of tens of kilometres per second that no gravitational encounter within the solar system could have produced. That is what Pan-STARRS saw in the orbit of a faint, fast-moving speck in October 2017 — and it is why, within days, astronomers knew they were looking at something that had never been catalogued before.

## 1I/'Oumuamua: the anomaly

### Caught on the way out

The object that became 1I/'Oumuamua — from the Hawaiian, roughly *a messenger from far away arriving first* — was found by the **Pan-STARRS 1** survey telescope on Haleakalā on 19 October 2017. By the time it was found it had already rounded the Sun. Perihelion had passed on 9 September, inside the orbit of Mercury at about **0.25 AU**, and the object was already outbound, receding and fading. The window was cruel: it was observable for only a couple of weeks before it dropped below the reach of even the largest telescopes. Everything we know about 'Oumuamua was extracted from that fortnight of a fading dot, and the wringing of information from so little is one of the small heroic episodes of modern observational astronomy.

The orbit came first and it was decisive. The eccentricity was about **1.20** — not marginally hyperbolic, where a passing planet might be blamed, but emphatically so. The excess velocity was **26 km/s**, matching the drift of the local stars almost eerily well. Run the trajectory backward and 'Oumuamua had been coasting through the galaxy at that speed for an unknown but very long time, and the Sun simply happened to be in its way.

{{image: Oumuamua | An artist's impression of 1I/'Oumuamua, reconstructed from its extreme brightness variations as an elongated, tumbling body. No telescope resolved it; every image of 'Oumuamua is an interpretation of a point of light that brightened and dimmed by a factor of ten as it rotated.}}

### Three anomalies, not one

Had 'Oumuamua been an ordinary rock on a hyperbolic orbit, it would have been a headline for a week and a footnote thereafter: *first interstellar object, looks like an asteroid, goodbye.* It refused to be ordinary in three separate ways, and it is the combination that made it the most-discussed small body of the decade.

**First, the shape.** No telescope resolved 'Oumuamua — it was always a single point of light — but that point of light *varied*, and dramatically. Its brightness swung by about **2.5 magnitudes**, a factor of ten, on a rotation period of several hours. A rotating body modulates its brightness because it presents a changing cross-section to us: broadside it is bright, end-on it is faint. A brightness range of a factor of ten implies a projected-area range of a factor of ten, and that implies a body extraordinarily far from round — an elongation of at least six to one, and quite possibly ten to one. Nothing in the solar system's asteroid population is shaped like that. The reconstructions that filled the world's newspapers — a dark, reddish, tumbling splinter, or on some analyses a flattened disc, a *pancake* rather than a cigar — were exactly that, reconstructions, but the underlying fact was solid: **whatever 'Oumuamua was, it was geometrically extreme.**

**Second, the tumble.** The lightcurve was not the clean, repeating signal of a body spinning about a single axis. It was consistent with **non-principal-axis rotation** — tumbling, the chaotic wobble of an object that has been knocked out of simple spin and not yet relaxed. That is the natural state of a collisional fragment, a chip knocked off something larger.

**Third, and most consequential: it accelerated.** When the two weeks of positions were fitted to a pure gravitational orbit, the fit *failed* — subtly, but well beyond the errors. 'Oumuamua was being pushed by a small extra force, directed away from the Sun and falling off roughly as $1/r^2$, that added a few parts in a thousand to its motion. In the solar system this signature is utterly familiar and utterly diagnostic: it is **cometary outgassing**. A comet warmed by the Sun vents gas from its sunward face, and the recoil nudges the orbit. The non-gravitational acceleration of comets is textbook.

Except that comets that outgas enough to shove their own orbits around are *visibly* doing so. They grow a coma, a fuzzy envelope of gas and dust; they grow a tail; they can be seen to be losing material. 'Oumuamua showed **none of it.** Deep images revealed no coma. The Spitzer Space Telescope stared at it in the infrared and detected no thermal glow from dust, no carbon monoxide, no carbon dioxide, setting stringent upper limits on any ordinary outgassing. Here was the knot: **an acceleration that in every solar-system precedent means outgassing, with no outgassing to be seen.**

### The candidate explanations, laid out fairly

An honest chapter lays the options on the table before judging them, so here they are, from the most conservative to the most radical. All of them are attempts to answer one question: *what pushes a small body outward without producing a coma we could detect?*

**A hydrogen iceberg.** Molecular hydrogen, $\mathrm{H_2}$, freezes at a few kelvin. A body made partly of solid hydrogen would sublimate under sunlight and produce thrust, but the vented $\mathrm{H_2}$ is transparent, monatomic-light, and carries no dust — an essentially **invisible** outgassing. It would also, as it eroded preferentially on the sunward side, tend to be sculpted into an elongated or flattened shape, killing two anomalies with one mechanism. The difficulty is the raw material. Solid hydrogen is exotic; it needs temperatures of a few kelvin to form and is fragile against starlight and cosmic rays. Critics argued that an $\mathrm{H_2}$ iceberg small enough to be 'Oumuamua would **evaporate during the journey** between star-forming clouds, never surviving to reach us. The idea is not dead, but it must first solve the problem of how the ice lives long enough.

**A nitrogen-ice fragment.** Nitrogen, $\mathrm{N_2}$, is the ice that coats **Pluto** and **Triton** — bright, dense, and, like hydrogen, sublimating into a gas that is hard to see and drags almost no dust. Suppose 'Oumuamua were a **chip knocked off the frozen surface of a Pluto-like body** in some other system, ejected by an impact. Nitrogen-ice sublimation would supply the invisible thrust; the high albedo of $\mathrm{N_2}$ ice fits the size and brightness; and preferential erosion of a slab would naturally flatten it toward the pancake reconstruction. This proposal has the great virtue of invoking a material we *know* exists in abundance on real bodies. Its difficulty is a **mass-budget** one: to seed the galaxy with enough $\mathrm{N_2}$ chips to make a detection like 'Oumuamua likely, other planetary systems would need to have made and shattered an enormous number of Pluto-like worlds — arguably more than the raw materials allow. The explanation is physically clean but demographically expensive.

**An ultra-porous fractal aggregate.** Suppose 'Oumuamua were not solid at all but a **fluffy, fractal cluster of dust** — an aggregate with a density less than that of air, mostly empty space, of the kind that can form as ices sublimate away and leave a refractory skeleton behind. Such a body has an enormous surface area for its mass, so **ordinary sunlight pressure** — the momentum of the photons themselves — would push it outward measurably, producing the observed $1/r^2$ acceleration with **no outgassing at all**. Radiation pressure alone does the job. The catch is structural: an aggregate that flimsy would struggle to survive its own spin and the tidal stress of a close perihelion pass without disintegrating, and it is a delicate thing to have crossed the galaxy intact.

**And a lightsail.** The most radical proposal, advanced seriously by a Harvard astronomer, took the same physics as the fractal aggregate and gave it a maker. A body pushed by sunlight against a mere $1/r^2$ needs a very high area-to-mass ratio — it must be **thin and flat and light**, exactly the specification of an artificial **lightsail**, a sheet built by a technological civilisation to ride starlight. The extreme shape, the lack of outgassing, and the radiation-pressure acceleration are, on this reading, not three awkward coincidences but three predictions of a single artificial hypothesis. 'Oumuamua, the argument ran, might be a piece of alien technology — a derelict sail, or a probe.

This is where the chapter stops being about ices and becomes about method.

### How to handle an anomaly

The lightsail claim is the most instructive thing about 'Oumuamua, not because it is likely — it is not — but because reacting to it correctly requires a discipline that the whole of this book has been building toward. The temptation, on hearing that one hypothesis accounts for all three anomalies at once, is to feel that the anomalies are therefore *evidence for* that hypothesis. They are not. Not yet. And the reason is a principle worth stating flatly:

> An observation that a hypothesis explains is not evidence **for** that hypothesis until the alternatives have been searched as hard.

The ocean-worlds chapter drew exactly this line in a different setting and insisted on it: a moon with the *ingredients* for life is **habitable**, and habitable is not **inhabited**; you may not infer the second, thrilling conclusion from the first, ordinary one just because the second would be more exciting. The same logic governs 'Oumuamua. An object with the *symptoms* of strangeness is **anomalous**, and anomalous is not **artificial**. The gap between them is the space of natural explanations you have not yet ruled out — and that space, as the four candidates above show, was not empty. It was crowded.

Make the reasoning quantitative, in the Bayesian shape it really has. What we want is the probability that 'Oumuamua is artificial *given* the data, $P(\text{artificial}\mid\text{data})$. Bayes' theorem says this is proportional to the likelihood times the prior:

$$P(\text{artificial}\mid\text{data}) \propto P(\text{data}\mid\text{artificial})\, P(\text{artificial})$$

The lightsail argument concentrates entirely on the first factor: it shows $P(\text{data}\mid\text{artificial})$ is high — a sail would indeed look like this. But a high likelihood is not a high posterior. Two other things dominate the answer. The first is the **prior**, $P(\text{artificial})$, which for "the first interstellar object we ever detected happens to be manufactured hardware" is, on any honest accounting, minuscule — this is the **base rate**, and extraordinary claims are extraordinary precisely because their base rate is low. The second is the competing likelihood $P(\text{data}\mid\text{natural})$, which the four natural hypotheses show is **not** small: nitrogen ice, hydrogen ice, and fluffy aggregates each reproduce the data too, and each rests on materials or processes we already know occur in nature. When the natural likelihood is comparable and the natural prior is overwhelmingly larger, the posterior is not close. The rational conclusion is: *probably a natural object of a kind we had not catalogued, and we should go find another one.*

None of this is a demand to stop looking, and it is important to be fair here rather than merely dismissive. The value of the lightsail proposal was real: it forced the field to articulate, precisely, what a technological object *would* look like, and to confront how thin our natural explanations still were. That is a service. The error is only in the last step — in treating a hypothesis that *could* explain the data as though it were *supported by* the data, before the mundane possibilities have been exhausted with the same energy. Openness and discipline are not opposites here. The discipline is what makes the openness worth anything: a field that will believe an anomaly is a spaceship on first sight is not open-minded, it is merely credulous, and it will never be trusted on the day the evidence is actually strong. Be genuinely willing to be surprised. Then search every ordinary explanation twice before you claim the extraordinary one. That is what handling an anomaly means, and 'Oumuamua is its case study.

The most useful next move was not another argument about 'Oumuamua. It was to find a **second** interstellar object and see whether it, too, was strange.

## 2I/Borisov: the reassurance of the ordinary

It came less than two years later, and it could not have been more different — in the best possible way.

On **30 August 2019** an amateur astronomer, **Gennadiy Borisov**, found a comet with a telescope he had built himself. Its orbit was hyperbolic beyond any doubt: eccentricity about **3.36**, far more extreme than 'Oumuamua's, with an excess velocity of some **32 km/s**. It was unmistakably the second interstellar object. And this time the timing was kind: 2I/Borisov was discovered **inbound**, months before its December perihelion at about 2 AU, giving the world's telescopes the better part of a year to study it properly — the opportunity 'Oumuamua had denied.

{{image: 2I/Borisov | 2I/Borisov imaged by the Hubble Space Telescope, showing a clear coma and tail. Unlike 'Oumuamua, the second interstellar object was an unambiguous, well-behaved comet — and its very ordinariness was the point.}}

What they found was a **comet**. A plain, unmistakable, textbook comet: a fuzzy coma, a dust tail, gas venting from a sublimating nucleus perhaps half a kilometre across. Spectra showed the familiar cometary molecules — cyanogen, then water — behaving exactly as an ordinary comet's should as it warmed toward the Sun. There was no mystery about what was pushing it or why it was fuzzy. It was fuzzy because it was outgassing, and it was outgassing because that is what comets do.

There was one genuinely interesting quirk. Borisov's coma was strikingly **rich in carbon monoxide** — its $\mathrm{CO/H_2O}$ ratio was high, near the top of the range seen among solar-system comets and beyond it. That points to a birthplace very cold and very far out in its home system, perhaps around a cooler star, where carbon monoxide could freeze onto the grains. A modest, legible piece of information about somewhere else. But a quirk within the normal range, not an anomaly outside it.

And that unremarkableness is precisely the result. Think about what two objects now told us. If interstellar space were full of 'Oumuamua-like weirdness, the second object should have been weird too. It was not. It was a normal comet, which is exactly what theory had predicted interstellar objects *should* mostly be — because comets, the volatile-rich planetesimals of a system's cold outskirts, are the easiest material for a planetary system to fling away, as the giant planets' gravity does routinely. Borisov made the population legible in a way 'Oumuamua alone never could: **most interstellar objects are probably ordinary comets, and 'Oumuamua's strangeness is the outlier, not the rule.** One object cannot tell you whether it is typical or exceptional. The second object can, and it did. 'Oumuamua did not become less interesting; it became more clearly *special*, which is a stronger scientific statement than "the first one we saw was weird."

## 3I/ATLAS: a visitor older than the Sun

The third came on **1 July 2025**, found by the **ATLAS** survey — the Asteroid Terrestrial-impact Last Alert System — from its station in Chile. If Borisov was the reassuring one, 3I/ATLAS was the *deep-time* one, and it may prove the most scientifically valuable of the three.

Its orbit set records. The eccentricity was **6.14** — far and away the most hyperbolic path yet measured for any known body — and it arrived at an excess velocity of about **58 km/s**, roughly twice 'Oumuamua's and the fastest of the three. That speed is not just a number on a chart; it is a fossil, and reading it is the heart of the object's story.

{{image: 3I/ATLAS | 3I/ATLAS, the third known interstellar object, discovered in 2025 on the most hyperbolic orbit ever measured. Its high velocity and trajectory link it kinematically to the Milky Way's ancient thick disk, implying a planetesimal that may be older than the solar system itself.}}

### Kinematics as a clock

Here is the chain of reasoning, and it is a lovely one. Stars in the galaxy are stirred over time. A star born recently, from cold gas in the galactic plane, moves with the calm, orderly flow of the disk and has a **low velocity** relative to its neighbours. But as billions of years pass, gravitational encounters with molecular clouds, spiral arms, and other stars gradually **heat** a star's orbit — nudging it onto a path that plunges above and below the plane and lags the local rotation. Old stars, on average, move **faster** relative to the local standard of rest than young ones. Velocity, statistically, is age. The fast-moving, puffed-up population is called the **thick disk**, and it is old — many billions of years, in places older than the Sun.

An interstellar object carries the velocity of the star that made it. So 3I/ATLAS's high speed and the particular direction of its approach place it, on the balance of the statistics, in that **thick-disk** population — which implies an age of order **seven to eleven billion years**, and quite possibly more. The Sun is 4.6 billion years old. On this reading 3I/ATLAS is a planetesimal that was **already ancient when the solar system formed** — a chip of some other system's construction that has been coasting through the galaxy for longer than the Earth has existed. We are, in the most literal sense, holding a piece of the deep past of the Milky Way in a telescope's field of view.

### Composition, in the right order

3I/ATLAS was a comet, and an active one, but it declared its ices in an unusual sequence — one that turns out to be a straightforward lesson in **sublimation physics**. Far from the Sun, while still beyond the orbit of Jupiter, its activity was dominated not by water but by **carbon dioxide**. This is exactly what the thermodynamics demands and is worth understanding rather than memorising. Water ice needs to be warmed to roughly 150 K before it sublimates briskly; at large heliocentric distances a comet is far too cold for that, and its water stays locked as solid. But $\mathrm{CO_2}$ is far more **volatile** — it sublimates vigorously at temperatures around 80 K, reached much farther out. So a comet coming in from the cold switches on its most volatile ices first. Early activity is carbon dioxide (and, for the coldest ices, carbon monoxide); water joins in only later, as the object crosses inside the distance where sunlight can finally boil it.

3I/ATLAS showed precisely this ordering. Space telescopes caught it venting **carbon dioxide at large distance**, a $\mathrm{CO_2}$-rich coma switched on while the Sun was still faint and cold in its sky, and only as it drew in toward its perihelion near 1.4 AU did **water sublimation** rise to take over. The sequence is a thermometer for the incoming journey and a compositional portrait of another system's outer disk all at once — and, notably, a $\mathrm{CO_2}$-rich portrait, another system's chemistry read off the order in which its ices ignited.

Three objects, then, three characters: the anomalous splinter, the ordinary comet, and the ancient $\mathrm{CO_2}$-rich wanderer possibly older than the Sun. Which raises the question that justifies the whole enterprise. What are they *for*?

## What they are for

Two answers, one about the individual object and one about the population.

### The only samples of other planetary systems we can watch

Astronomy studies other stars and other planets almost entirely by their **light** — a spectrum of a star, the dimming of a transit, the faint glow of a directly imaged giant. It is a science conducted at a distance of light-years, on photons that left their source long ago. Interstellar objects break that rule. They are **material** — actual condensed matter, ices and dust and rock, assembled in another star's disk — brought bodily into our own neighbourhood where our telescopes can dissect its composition, watch its ices sublimate in order, and measure its shape and spin. Short of an interstellar probe we will not launch this century, this is **the only way we will ever examine the solid building blocks of another planetary system directly.** Every spectrum of Borisov's coma, every sequence of 3I/ATLAS's ices, is a chemical analysis of a place we can never visit, delivered to our door. That alone would justify chasing them.

### Number density measures how efficiently systems throw material away

The deeper payoff is statistical, and it reaches a quantity nothing else can touch. Planet formation is not tidy. As a giant planet grows, its gravity does not merely accrete nearby planetesimals; it **scatters** them, flinging a large fraction of a disk's small bodies onto orbits that leave the system entirely. Our own solar system did this on a grand scale — the Oort cloud is the population that was flung *almost* free, and everything flung a little harder is gone. Every planetary system is, in this sense, a **cannon**, and the interstellar objects are the spent shot filling the galaxy between the stars.

How efficient is the cannon? What fraction of a system's solid mass gets ejected rather than kept? This is a first-order question about how planets form, and it is essentially **unmeasurable by any other means** — you cannot watch a distant system's ejecta, because they are dark, cold, and scattered across parsecs. But you *can* measure the **number density** of interstellar objects passing through the solar system, because you can count how many you catch and how big a volume you swept to catch them. And that density is a direct constraint on the ejection efficiency, because every one of those objects came from some star's cannon.

The arithmetic, even done crudely, is startling, and it is worked in the problems. The density implied by 'Oumuamua's single detection is of order **0.1 object per cubic AU** for bodies of its size and larger — which, divided among the stars that must have made them, implies each star has ejected something like an **Earth mass or more** of hundred-metre-and-larger planetesimals, and, once the size distribution is accounted for, plausibly **many Earth masses** of solid material thrown away for every star in the galaxy. That is a demanding number. It says planetary systems are prodigiously wasteful — that the material retained in planets is a minority of what the disk made, the rest exiled to interstellar space. And it is exactly here that **Borisov earns its keep a second time**: a galaxy seeded mostly with ordinary comets is a galaxy whose cannons fired *comets*, the volatile-rich outer-disk planetesimals that are easiest to scatter — which is comfortably consistent with how we understand ejection to work. The population's composition and its number density tell the same story, and the story is that forming planets is an act of enormous throwing-away.

## The detection problem, and why it is about to end

For all their importance, three objects in eight years is a starvation diet, and the reason is a trio of brutal observational facts.

**They are fast.** An object crossing at tens of kilometres per second does not linger. It is in the observable inner solar system for weeks to a few months, not the years a bound comet grants, and it never comes back. Miss the window and it is gone from the universe, not merely from this apparition.

**They are faint.** They are small — hundreds of metres to a couple of kilometres — and they spend nearly all their time far from the Sun where they are dark and cold. They brighten to detectability only briefly, near perihelion, and even then they are near the limit of survey telescopes.

**They are usually found on the way out.** This is the cruellest. 'Oumuamua was discovered a month and a half *after* perihelion, already outbound and fading, because the sky is large and a fast faint mover is easy to miss on the way in. A post-perihelion discovery is the worst case: it maximises the object's distance and minimises the time left to study it. The single most valuable thing a survey could do is catch these objects **inbound**, weeks or months before perihelion, while they are approaching and there is time to organise the world's telescopes — and, one day, a spacecraft.

That is exactly the capability now coming online. The **Vera C. Rubin Observatory** in Chile, with its 8.4-metre mirror and a 3,200-megapixel camera imaging the entire visible southern sky every few nights, is beginning its **Legacy Survey of Space and Time** — a decade-long, wide, fast, deep movie of the sky. Its whole design is a machine for catching faint fast-movers, and its cadence means it should sweep up interstellar objects at a rate that turns a trickle into a stream, plausibly several to many per year, and crucially many of them caught **inbound**, with lead time to react. Rubin also feeds an **alert stream** and a **target-of-opportunity** apparatus built to flag exactly such transients within minutes and route them to follow-up. The bottleneck on interstellar science has never been the objects; the galaxy is full of them. The bottleneck was our ability to notice them in time. That bottleneck is about to open.

{{image: Vera C. Rubin Observatory | The Vera C. Rubin Observatory in Chile, whose Legacy Survey of Space and Time is designed to image the entire southern sky every few nights. Its cadence is expected to transform the interstellar-object discovery rate from a handful per decade to a stream, and to catch many of them inbound with time to spare.}}

## Comet Interceptor: a mission for an object that does not exist yet

There is one more move, and it is audacious. Everything so far has been done with telescopes from a distance. But we have flown spacecraft *to* comets — orbited them, landed on them, sampled their dust. Could we do that to an interstellar object, and analyse a piece of another solar system *in situ*?

The obstacle is the very speed and unpredictability that define these objects. You cannot build a spacecraft, design its trajectory, and launch it toward a target that has not been discovered — and by the time an interstellar object *is* discovered, it is weeks from perihelion and moving at tens of kilometres per second, far too late to build anything. The conventional mission sequence — find the target, then plan the mission — cannot work here. The target arrives, and leaves, faster than a mission can be conceived.

The European Space Agency's answer, **Comet Interceptor**, inverts the sequence. Build the spacecraft *first*, launch it with **no target at all**, and park it in a stable **waiting orbit** at the **Sun-Earth $L_2$** point — the gravitational balance point some 1.5 million kilometres out along the Earth's night side, the same quiet perch used by the great space telescopes — where it can loiter for years, holding its station and its fuel, poised. Then, when Rubin or another survey finds a suitable target inbound with enough warning — ideally a pristine long-period comet on its first visit from the Oort cloud, or, the prize, a genuine **interstellar object** — the parked spacecraft drops out of its waiting orbit and flies to intercept, releasing sub-probes to pass through the coma and image the nucleus from multiple angles at once.

It is a mission designed, quite deliberately, for **an object that does not yet exist** — or rather, that exists somewhere out in the dark but has not yet been found. That is a genuinely new way to do deep-space science: not *find, then chase*, but **wait, poised, until the galaxy sends something worth chasing.** It is the logical endpoint of everything this chapter has taught — that these objects are real, are common, are precious, and are catchable if only you are ready in time. Comet Interceptor is what being ready looks like.

{{image: Comet Interceptor | Comet Interceptor, an ESA mission that will launch with no target and wait in a parking orbit at the Sun-Earth $L_2$ point until a survey finds a pristine comet — or an interstellar object — worth intercepting. It is a spacecraft built for an object that has not yet been discovered.}}

## Pulling the thread

Everything in this chapter descends from a single circumstance: **planetary systems do not keep everything they make.** They form fast, they form violently, and their growing giant planets scatter a large fraction of the leftover planetesimals clean out of the system. The galaxy is therefore not empty between the stars; it is thinly seeded with the ejected building blocks of every planetary system that ever formed. From that one fact the rest follows:

- Because these objects are **unbound**, they arrive on **hyperbolic** orbits with a residual **excess velocity** of tens of kilometres per second — a signature no solar-system encounter can fake, and the entry ticket to the whole class.
- Because they are **direct samples** of other systems, each one is a chemical analysis of a place we can never visit — Borisov's carbon monoxide, 3I/ATLAS's carbon dioxide igniting before its water, a portrait of another disk's cold outskirts read off in order.
- Because their velocity encodes their **age**, 3I/ATLAS's speed marks it as a fragment of the ancient thick disk, plausibly **older than the Sun** — deep galactic time delivered to the inner solar system.
- Because their **number density** is a measurable proxy for a quantity nothing else can reach, counting them constrains **how efficiently planetary systems eject material** — and the answer, demanding as it is, says forming planets means throwing most of the solids away.
- And because 'Oumuamua was strange enough to invite an extraordinary claim, the population also teaches **method** — that an anomaly is not evidence for the exotic until the mundane has been exhausted, and that the reply to the first weird object is not a press conference but a **second object**, which is exactly what Borisov provided.

We stand, on interstellar objects, roughly where astronomy stood on exoplanets in the early 1990s: a category just born, a bare handful of members, and a survey machine about to convert a curiosity into a statistical science. The next decade will very likely turn these three characters into a catalogue of hundreds, with masses and ages and compositions — a census of the debris of other worlds. And somewhere out in the dark, on a hyperbola that has not yet bent, the target for which Comet Interceptor is waiting is already on its way.

## Further reading

The primary literature on all three objects is unusually accessible, because each was a rapid, public, collaborative effort played out in near-real time. The discovery and characterisation papers on 'Oumuamua — the orbit, the lightcurve, and the disputed non-gravitational acceleration — are the place to see how much can be wrung from two weeks of a fading point of light, and how carefully the outgassing limits were set. For the debate over what 'Oumuamua was, read the natural-explanation papers (the nitrogen-ice and hydrogen-ice proposals and their critiques) alongside the artificial one, and notice that the disagreement is not about the data but about the priors. For the population and the detection future, the Rubin Observatory's own survey documentation lays out the expected interstellar-object yield, and ESA's Comet Interceptor pages describe the wait-and-pounce architecture in detail. The one popular book worth naming argues the artificial case at length and is best read *against* the discipline this chapter has urged — as an exercise in separating a hypothesis that could explain the data from one the data actually support. {{book: Avi Loeb | Extraterrestrial: The First Sign of Intelligent Life Beyond Earth | 2021}}

- **Loeb, Avi, *Extraterrestrial: The First Sign of Intelligent Life Beyond Earth* (2021).** The lightsail case for 'Oumuamua argued forcefully by its leading proponent — valuable read critically, as a case study in how a low prior and a high likelihood combine.

## Problems

*Useful constants and figures: $GM_\odot = 1.327\times10^{20}\ \mathrm{m^3/s^2}$; 1 AU $=1.496\times10^{11}$ m; 1 pc $=206{,}265$ AU $=3.086\times10^{16}$ m; local stellar number density $\approx 0.1\ \mathrm{pc^{-3}}$; Earth mass $=5.97\times10^{24}$ kg. A brightness ratio corresponds to a magnitude difference by $\Delta m = 2.5\log_{10}(\text{ratio})$. Problems 4 and 6 are the conceptual core — 4 is the anomaly-discipline problem and 6 the ejection-efficiency argument; problem 9 has no settled answer and is meant to be argued.*

**1 — The entry ticket.** **(a)** Explain, in terms of orbital energy, why an eccentricity greater than one guarantees an object is not bound to the Sun. **(b)** 'Oumuamua had $e\approx1.20$, Borisov $e\approx3.36$, 3I/ATLAS $e\approx6.14$. Which travelled fastest relative to the Sun far from perihelion, and why can you tell from eccentricity alone only with an added assumption? **(c)** Why is a hyperbolic excess velocity of tens of km/s, rather than the eccentricity by itself, the physically fundamental signature of an interstellar origin?

**2 — Reading the excess velocity.** For a hyperbolic orbit the semi-major axis is negative and $v_\infty=\sqrt{GM_\odot/|a|}$. **(a)** 3I/ATLAS arrived with $v_\infty\approx58$ km/s. Compute $|a|$ in AU. **(b)** 'Oumuamua's $v_\infty\approx26$ km/s. Compute its $|a|$ and comment on the sign and magnitude compared with a bound comet. **(c)** 'Oumuamua's incoming velocity relative to the Sun was strikingly close to the average motion of nearby stars. What does that near-match suggest about its age relative to 3I/ATLAS, whose velocity was much larger?

**3 — The shape from the lightcurve.** 'Oumuamua's brightness varied by $\Delta m\approx2.5$ magnitudes as it rotated. **(a)** Convert this to a ratio of maximum to minimum projected area. **(b)** For a rotating elongated body viewed in its equatorial plane, that area ratio equals the ratio of its long to short axis. Estimate the axis ratio. **(c)** Explain why the value you found is a **lower** limit on the true elongation — that is, why the real body is at least this stretched and possibly more. **(d)** What does the tumbling (non-principal-axis) rotation independently suggest about 'Oumuamua's history?

**4 — The discipline of the anomaly (conceptual core).** 'Oumuamua showed a non-gravitational acceleration with no detectable coma, and the lightsail hypothesis explains this cleanly. **(a)** Write Bayes' theorem for $P(\text{artificial}\mid\text{data})$ and identify which factor the lightsail argument establishes and which two factors it tends to neglect. **(b)** Explain, using the numbers of the situation, why a high $P(\text{data}\mid\text{artificial})$ does not translate into a high posterior. **(c)** Name at least two natural hypotheses that also make $P(\text{data}\mid\text{natural})$ non-negligible, and state the specific weakness of each. **(d)** The ocean-worlds chapter insisted that *habitable* is not *inhabited*. State the exact analogue for 'Oumuamua and explain why the two cautions are the same reasoning. **(e)** Was the lightsail proposal therefore worthless? Argue both what it got wrong and what it contributed.

**5 — Why Borisov mattered.** 2I/Borisov was an unremarkable comet. **(a)** Explain why an *unremarkable* second object was a more powerful result than a second weird one would have been. **(b)** Borisov's coma was unusually rich in carbon monoxide. What does that indicate about where it formed, and why? **(c)** Theory predicted most interstellar objects should be comets rather than 'Oumuamua-like bodies. Give the physical reason, tied to how planetary systems eject material.

**6 — Counting the cannon's shot (ejection efficiency).** Take the space density of interstellar objects of 'Oumuamua's size and larger to be $n\approx0.1\ \mathrm{AU^{-3}}$, the local stellar density $0.1\ \mathrm{pc^{-3}}$, and a characteristic mass per object of $3\times10^8$ kg. **(a)** Convert $n$ to objects per cubic parsec. **(b)** Dividing by the stellar density, estimate the number of such objects each star must have ejected, on average. **(c)** Multiply by the mass per object to estimate the ejected mass of hundred-metre-and-larger bodies per star, in Earth masses. **(d)** The true total ejected mass is larger still. Why? And what does the size of this number imply about planet formation? **(e)** Explain why this ejection efficiency is essentially impossible to measure by any means *other* than counting interstellar objects.

**7 — The thermometer of the ices.** 3I/ATLAS vented carbon dioxide while still far from the Sun, and water only later. **(a)** Explain the ordering in terms of the different sublimation temperatures of $\mathrm{CO_2}$ and $\mathrm{H_2O}$. **(b)** Roughly why does a more volatile ice switch on at a larger heliocentric distance? Sketch the temperature-versus-distance reasoning. **(c)** Contrast this with 'Oumuamua's acceleration, which showed *no* detectable gas at all. What kinds of ice could plausibly drive a comet-like thrust while remaining invisible, and why?

**8 — Why we keep missing them.** **(a)** List the three observational properties that make interstellar objects hard to catch, and explain which is the most damaging for follow-up science and why. **(b)** Why is an *inbound* discovery so much more valuable than an *outbound* one? Use 'Oumuamua as the cautionary example. **(c)** Explain the design logic of parking Comet Interceptor at the Sun-Earth $L_2$ point and launching it with no assigned target. What problem does this solve that a conventional "find it, then build the mission" approach cannot?

**9 — Open problem: was 'Oumuamua unique?** With only three objects known, we cannot yet say whether 'Oumuamua's strangeness — extreme shape, no visible outgassing, non-gravitational acceleration — is a rare freak or the tip of a common but hard-to-detect population. **(a)** State the two competing possibilities precisely. **(b)** Borisov and 3I/ATLAS were both ordinary comets; does that settle the question? Argue carefully about selection effects. **(c)** Design the observational programme that would most cleanly decide whether 'Oumuamua-type objects are common, being specific about what the Rubin Observatory would need to measure and what each hypothesis predicts for the *ratio* of 'Oumuamua-like to Borisov-like discoveries. **(d)** Suppose Rubin finds twenty interstellar objects over a decade and none resembles 'Oumuamua. What would you then conclude, and what would still be unprovable?

## Worked answers

**1 — The entry ticket.** **(a)** The specific orbital energy is $\varepsilon = -GM_\odot/2a$, and it is also $\varepsilon = \tfrac{1}{2}v^2 - GM_\odot/r$. A bound orbit has $\varepsilon<0$: the kinetic energy is never enough to reach infinity, so the object always falls back. Eccentricity and energy are linked through $a$: $e<1$ corresponds to $a>0$ and $\varepsilon<0$ (bound ellipse); $e=1$ to a parabola with $\varepsilon=0$ (marginally unbound); $e>1$ to $a<0$ and $\varepsilon>0$ (hyperbola, unbound). So $e>1$ *is* the statement that total energy is positive — the object has more than escape energy and cannot be held. **(b)** Far from perihelion, speed approaches $v_\infty=\sqrt{GM_\odot/|a|}$, which depends on $|a|$, not directly on $e$ alone; $e$ and $q$ (perihelion) together fix $a$ through $q=a(1-e)$. With the *added assumption* of comparable perihelion distances, the larger eccentricity implies the smaller $|a|$ and hence the larger $v_\infty$ — so 3I/ATLAS ($e=6.14$), which indeed had the highest measured $v_\infty\approx58$ km/s, was fastest, followed by Borisov and then 'Oumuamua. But eccentricity alone is insufficient without perihelion, because a very eccentric orbit with a large perihelion can have a modest $v_\infty$. **(c)** Because $v_\infty$ is the object's true cruising speed through interstellar space — its velocity relative to the Sun stripped of the gravitational boost — and tens of km/s matches the drift of neighbouring stars while exceeding anything a solar-system encounter could impart. Eccentricity is a derived, orbit-shape quantity; $v_\infty$ is the physical velocity that identifies the object as a free galactic body rather than a bound one perturbed onto a barely-hyperbolic path.

**2 — Reading the excess velocity.** **(a)** $|a| = GM_\odot/v_\infty^2 = (1.327\times10^{20})/(5.8\times10^4)^2 = 1.327\times10^{20}/3.364\times10^9 = 3.95\times10^{10}$ m $= 0.264$ AU. **(b)** For 'Oumuamua, $|a| = 1.327\times10^{20}/(2.6\times10^4)^2 = 1.327\times10^{20}/6.76\times10^8 = 1.96\times10^{11}$ m $=1.31$ AU. The semi-major axis is *negative* ($a\approx-1.3$ AU for 'Oumuamua, $a\approx-0.26$ AU for 3I/ATLAS) — a formal marker of a hyperbola, since a real bound comet has $a$ positive and often hundreds or thousands of AU. The small $|a|$ reflects the high excess speed: the faster the object, the *smaller* the magnitude of its (negative) semi-major axis. **(c)** The near-match of 'Oumuamua's incoming velocity to the average motion of local stars means it was moving with the bulk flow of the solar neighbourhood — dynamically "cold," not heated onto a fast, plunging orbit. Since orbits are heated over time, a low velocity relative to the local standard suggests a *younger* population, whereas 3I/ATLAS's much larger velocity marks it as dynamically hot and therefore old — thick-disk, billions of years, plausibly older than the Sun. Velocity is, statistically, a clock.

**3 — The shape from the lightcurve.** **(a)** Area ratio $=10^{\,\Delta m/2.5}=10^{2.5/2.5}=10^{1.0}=10$. The body presents about ten times more area at its brightest than at its faintest. **(b)** For an elongated ellipsoid rotating and viewed edge-on to its spin axis, maximum area $\propto$ long axis $a$ and minimum area $\propto$ short axis $b$, so $a/b\approx10$ — an elongation of order ten to one (careful lightcurve modelling, allowing for tumbling and unknown viewing geometry, yields a robust lower bound nearer six to one). Either way, geometrically extreme: nothing in the asteroid belt is shaped like this. **(c)** It is a lower limit because the full amplitude is seen *only* if we happen to view the rotation equator-on. If the spin axis is tilted toward us, the presented area varies less and the observed amplitude *underestimates* the true elongation. Since we did not know the aspect and had no reason to expect the most favourable one, the real body is *at least* this stretched and possibly more — the inequality runs in the direction of greater weirdness, not less. **(d)** Non-principal-axis rotation (tumbling) is the signature of a body that has been dynamically excited — knocked out of simple spin, for instance by a collision, and not yet damped back to relaxation (which for a small, rigid body takes a very long time). It points to 'Oumuamua being a **fragment** — a chip off something larger — consistent with the collisional-shard picture behind several of the natural explanations.

**4 — The discipline of the anomaly.** **(a)** $P(\text{artificial}\mid\text{data}) \propto P(\text{data}\mid\text{artificial})\,P(\text{artificial})$. The lightsail argument establishes the **likelihood** $P(\text{data}\mid\text{artificial})$ — a thin, flat sail *would* reproduce the shape, the missing coma, and the radiation-pressure acceleration. It neglects the **prior** $P(\text{artificial})$ — the base rate of manufactured hardware being the first interstellar object we detect, which is minuscule — and the **competing likelihood** $P(\text{data}\mid\text{natural})$, which is not small. **(b)** A posterior is likelihood times prior, normalised against the alternatives. Even if $P(\text{data}\mid\text{artificial})$ is near one, multiplying by a vanishingly small prior and dividing by a natural branch that also fits the data well leaves the artificial posterior tiny. Concretely: natural mechanisms (nitrogen ice, hydrogen ice, fluffy aggregate) each reproduce the data using materials we know exist, so their combined $P(\text{data}\mid\text{natural})P(\text{natural})$ dwarfs the artificial term despite the artificial likelihood being high. High likelihood is necessary, not sufficient. **(c)** *Nitrogen-ice fragment*: invisible sublimation and pancake shape fit, but requires other systems to have made and shattered an implausibly large number of Pluto-like bodies — a mass-budget problem. *Hydrogen-ice iceberg*: invisible thrust and self-sculpting fit, but solid $\mathrm{H_2}$ likely evaporates during the interstellar journey and may never survive to arrive. *Ultra-porous fractal aggregate*: radiation pressure alone gives the acceleration with no outgassing, but such a fragile structure is hard to keep intact across the galaxy and through perihelion. Each is imperfect — but each keeps $P(\text{data}\mid\text{natural})$ well above zero, which is all the argument needs. **(d)** The exact analogue: *anomalous is not artificial*, just as *habitable is not inhabited*. In both cases one is tempted to leap from an ordinary, weaker claim (this environment has the ingredients / this object has strange symptoms) to an extraordinary, stronger one (there is life here / this is a spaceship) simply because the stronger claim is more exciting and *could* account for the observation. Both cautions are the same refusal to infer a low-prior conclusion from data that a high-prior conclusion also explains — the discipline of not letting the likelihood do the prior's job. **(e)** Not worthless. It got the *last step* wrong: it treated a hypothesis that could explain the data as though the data supported it, before the natural options were exhausted with equal energy — a prior-neglect error. But it contributed genuinely: it forced the field to specify precisely what a technological object would look like, exposed how thin the natural explanations still were, and drove real work on nitrogen and hydrogen ices. The right verdict is to prize the openness and reject the conclusion — and to note that the productive response to the whole affair was not to argue harder but to find a second object.

**5 — Why Borisov mattered.** **(a)** One object cannot tell you whether it is typical or exceptional — with a sample of one, "weird" and "representative" are indistinguishable. The second object breaks that degeneracy. Because Borisov was an *ordinary comet*, it revealed that interstellar objects are not generally 'Oumuamua-like, which simultaneously (i) confirmed the theoretical expectation that most should be comets and (ii) reclassified 'Oumuamua as a genuine *outlier* rather than a representative sample. "The first one was weird" is a weak statement; "the population is ordinary and the first was the exception" is a strong one, and only the second object could license it. **(b)** A high $\mathrm{CO/H_2O}$ ratio indicates formation in a very cold, distant region of its home system — carbon monoxide is so volatile it only freezes onto grains at very low temperatures, far from the star (or around a cooler star). Its abundance is a thermometer for the birthplace, pointing to the frigid outer reaches of another disk. **(c)** Planetary systems eject material chiefly by gravitational scattering from growing giant planets, and the bodies most easily scattered are those in the outer disk, loosely bound and volatile-rich — i.e. **comets**. The retained, tightly-bound inner bodies are hard to eject; the loosely-held outer icy planetesimals are flung away in bulk. So the ejected population — the interstellar objects — should be comet-dominated, exactly as Borisov exemplified.

**6 — Counting the cannon's shot.** **(a)** $1\ \mathrm{pc}=206{,}265$ AU, so $1\ \mathrm{pc^3}=(2.06265\times10^5)^3\ \mathrm{AU^3}=8.78\times10^{15}\ \mathrm{AU^3}$. Then $n=0.1\ \mathrm{AU^{-3}}=0.1\times8.78\times10^{15}=8.78\times10^{14}$ objects per pc³. **(b)** Objects per star $= n/(\text{stellar density}) = 8.78\times10^{14}/0.1 = 8.78\times10^{15}\approx10^{16}$ such objects ejected per star. **(c)** Ejected mass per star $\approx 10^{16}\times3\times10^8\ \mathrm{kg}=3\times10^{24}$ kg $=3\times10^{24}/5.97\times10^{24}\approx0.5$ Earth mass — in *hundred-metre-and-larger* bodies alone. **(d)** The true total is larger because a realistic size distribution places a great deal of additional mass in bodies both larger (kilometre-scale and up) and, depending on the slope, in the swarm of smaller ones not counted here; a full accounting readily reaches *many* Earth masses of solids ejected per star. That is a large fraction of — or more than — the solid mass a typical disk contains, implying planet formation is **prodigiously wasteful**: most of the solid building material is scattered out of the system rather than retained in planets. **(e)** Because ejected material is dark, cold, and dispersed across parsecs of interstellar space — invisible around its parent star and undetectable at a distance. The only place you can *count* it is where it passes close enough to be seen, i.e. transiting our own solar system. Measuring the local number density and dividing by the local stellar density is the sole route to the ejection efficiency; there is no remote-sensing substitute.

**7 — The thermometer of the ices.** **(a)** $\mathrm{CO_2}$ is far more volatile than $\mathrm{H_2O}$: it sublimates briskly at roughly 80 K, whereas water ice needs about 150 K to sublimate vigorously. As a comet falls sunward and warms, it therefore reaches the $\mathrm{CO_2}$-sublimation temperature first — while still far out and cold — and only later, closer in, gets hot enough to boil off water. Hence carbon dioxide leads and water follows. **(b)** Equilibrium temperature falls with heliocentric distance roughly as $T\propto r^{-1/2}$ (radiative balance with sunlight diluted as $r^{-2}$). A more volatile ice reaches its sublimation temperature at a *lower* $T$, which corresponds to a *larger* $r$. So plotting the (declining) temperature against distance, each ice "switches on" where the curve crosses its own sublimation threshold: $\mathrm{CO_2}$'s low threshold is crossed far out, water's higher threshold only much closer in. The volatility ordering of the ices maps directly onto a distance ordering of activity. **(c)** 'Oumuamua's thrust with no visible gas requires an ice that sublimates into something essentially *undetectable* — no dust dragged along, no bright emission bands, low column. Candidates are **molecular hydrogen** and **molecular nitrogen**: both sublimate into transparent, hard-to-detect gas that carries little or no dust, so they could push the body while leaving no coma the telescopes could catch — unlike water or $\mathrm{CO_2}$, whose comae and dust are readily seen (as in Borisov and 3I/ATLAS). The very invisibility that makes these ices attractive explanations is also what makes them hard to confirm.

**8 — Why we keep missing them.** **(a)** They are **fast** (in range for weeks to months, then gone forever), **faint** (small and usually far from the Sun, near survey limits), and **usually found on the way out** (post-perihelion). The most damaging is the last: a post-perihelion discovery maximises the object's distance and minimises the time remaining, gutting the chance for detailed follow-up — worse even than faintness, because it also forecloses any spacecraft response. **(b)** An inbound discovery, weeks or months before perihelion, gives lead time — to schedule the world's large telescopes, to plan spectroscopy across the approach, and in principle to launch or redirect a spacecraft. 'Oumuamua is the cautionary example: found a month and a half *after* perihelion, already outbound and fading, it was observable for only about two weeks, and every conclusion about it had to be squeezed from that fragment. Catching it inbound would have transformed the science. **(c)** Interstellar objects cannot be targeted in advance — they are discovered only weeks from perihelion, far too late to design and build a mission — yet by then they are moving too fast to reach with anything built afterward. Comet Interceptor solves this by **inverting the sequence**: build and launch first, with no target, and *wait* in a stable parking orbit at the Sun-Earth $L_2$ point, holding fuel and station for years until a survey finds a suitable inbound target with enough warning, then drop out and intercept. It converts an impossible "find, then build" problem into a workable "be ready, then pounce" one.

**9 — Open problem: was 'Oumuamua unique?** **(a)** Possibility one: 'Oumuamua-type objects (extreme shape, invisible outgassing, radiation-driven or exotic-ice acceleration) are genuinely **rare** — 'Oumuamua was a freak, and the population is overwhelmingly ordinary comets. Possibility two: such objects are actually **common** but **hard to detect** — small, dark, non-outgassing bodies are far fainter and less conspicuous than active comets, so surveys are strongly biased toward finding the comets and against finding the 'Oumuamuas, and the two we have seen since are a selection artefact rather than a true sample. **(b)** No, it does not settle it, precisely because of that selection effect. Borisov and 3I/ATLAS were both *active comets* — bright, fuzzy, and easy to discover — so finding two of them tells you comets are catchable, not that inert 'Oumuamua-like bodies are rare. An inactive splinter would be systematically harder to see, so its absence from a small sample is weak evidence. You must correct for detectability before comparing rates. **(c)** Use Rubin/LSST to build a large, *detectability-characterised* sample: for every interstellar object found, record its size, activity, and the survey's completeness for objects of that brightness and behaviour, so the raw counts can be de-biased into true relative abundances. The key measurement is the **ratio** of 'Oumuamua-like (inactive, non-outgassing, extreme-lightcurve) to Borisov-like (active, cometary) discoveries, *after* correcting each for how detectable it is. Possibility one predicts that even after correcting for the harder detectability of inert bodies, they remain a small minority. Possibility two predicts that once the correction is applied, inert 'Oumuamua-like objects are comparably or *more* numerous than comets — the comets merely dominate the raw, uncorrected counts. **(d)** Twenty objects, none resembling 'Oumuamua, would push toward possibility one: it would show 'Oumuamua-like objects are at most a modest minority of the *detectable* population, and if Rubin's completeness for inert bodies is well quantified, it would set a real upper limit on their true fraction. But it would remain unprovable that they are truly rare *in space*, because any survey has a faintness floor below which inert, non-outgassing bodies simply cannot be seen; a population of small dark splinters below that floor could still be abundant and forever undetected by this method. The honest conclusion would be a bounded statement — rare among detectable interstellar objects — not an unbounded one about what actually fills the galaxy.$astroISO_master$,
    true,
    true
  )
