  select $astroISO_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "1I/'Oumuamua (2017) showed a small non-gravitational acceleration directed away from the Sun and falling off roughly as 1/r^2. Why did this become the central puzzle of the object rather than a routine measurement?", "options": ["Because the acceleration was far too large to be produced by any known force, exceeding solar radiation pressure by orders of magnitude and implying an onboard engine", "Because the acceleration proved the orbit was actually bound, contradicting the hyperbolic eccentricity of about 1.20 that had admitted it to the interstellar class", "Because in every solar-system precedent that 1/r^2 outward push is the diagnostic signature of cometary outgassing, yet deep imaging showed no coma and Spitzer detected no dust, CO, or CO2 down to stringent limits", "Because the acceleration was intermittent, switching on and off in a way that no natural process could produce and that only active control could explain"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "The acceleration itself was tiny, only a few parts in a thousand, and its outward 1/r^2 form is utterly familiar: a comet warmed by the Sun vents gas from its sunward face and the recoil nudges the orbit. That is textbook. The knot is that comets which outgas enough to shove their own orbits are visibly doing so, growing a coma and a tail. 'Oumuamua showed none of it: deep images revealed no coma, and Spitzer set stringent upper limits on any ordinary outgassing (no dust, no CO, no CO2). So the puzzle was an acceleration that always means outgassing, with no outgassing to be seen. The other options misstate the physics: the push was small (not engine-scale), the orbit was emphatically unbound at e about 1.20, and the acceleration was a smooth 1/r^2 decline, not an intermittent switching signal."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "'Oumuamua's brightness swung by about 2.5 magnitudes (a factor of ten) over a rotation period of several hours. What does the chapter conclude from this lightcurve, and why is the conclusion a lower limit?", "options": ["That the body was at least six-to-one and possibly ten-to-one elongated, and this is only a lower limit because a spin axis tilted toward us would reduce the observed amplitude below the true elongation", "That the body was exactly ten-to-one elongated, a figure fixed precisely because the factor-of-ten brightness range maps one-to-one onto the axis ratio regardless of viewing angle", "That the body was rotating so fast it must be a solid metallic fragment, since only metal could hold together against the implied centrifugal stress", "That the body had a bright and a dark hemisphere, so the variation reflects albedo patches rather than shape and tells us nothing about elongation"], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "A rotating body modulates its brightness because it presents a changing cross-section: broadside it is bright, end-on it is faint. A factor-of-ten brightness range implies a factor-of-ten projected-area range, and for an elongated body viewed in its equatorial plane that equals the long-to-short axis ratio, so roughly ten to one, with careful modelling giving a robust lower bound near six to one. It is a lower limit because the full amplitude appears only if we happen to view the rotation equator-on; if the spin axis is tilted toward us the presented area varies less and the observed amplitude underestimates the true elongation. Since the aspect was unknown, the real body is at least this stretched and possibly more, so the inequality runs toward greater weirdness. The variation is a shape (cross-section) effect, not merely albedo patches, and the elongation is extreme by any solar-system standard."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The chapter lays out four candidate explanations for 'Oumuamua's outward acceleration without a visible coma. Which pairing correctly matches a candidate to its specific difficulty?", "options": ["The nitrogen-ice fragment fails because N2 gas is easily seen, so it should have produced a bright, obvious coma like Borisov's", "The ultra-porous fractal aggregate is driven by ordinary sunlight pressure with no outgassing at all, but such a flimsy, near-airless-density structure would struggle to survive its own spin and a close perihelion pass intact", "The hydrogen iceberg is ruled out because solid H2 is far too common in interstellar space to have produced anything as rare as 'Oumuamua", "The lightsail hypothesis is favoured because it is the only one of the four that can reproduce the extreme shape, the missing coma, and the radiation-pressure acceleration together"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The fractal-aggregate proposal supposes 'Oumuamua was a fluffy cluster of dust with a density less than that of air, an enormous surface area for its mass, so ordinary photon pressure alone pushes it outward at the observed 1/r^2 with no outgassing at all. Its catch is structural: an aggregate that flimsy would struggle to survive its own spin and the tidal stress of a close perihelion without disintegrating. The other pairings invert the actual arguments. Nitrogen ice sublimates into gas that is hard to see and drags almost no dust (its real difficulty is a mass budget: too many shattered Pluto-like worlds required). Solid H2 is exotic and fragile, and the objection is that it would evaporate during the interstellar journey, not that it is too common. And the lightsail is not uniquely capable: the fractal aggregate reproduces the same radiation-pressure physics naturally, which is exactly why the artificial hypothesis is not favoured."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "2I/Borisov (2019) was an unambiguous, well-behaved comet with a strikingly high CO/H2O ratio. Why does the chapter treat its very ordinariness as an important scientific result rather than an anticlimax?", "options": ["Because a second weird object would have confirmed 'Oumuamua, whereas an ordinary one left the first object's strangeness completely unexplained and therefore useless", "Because Borisov's hyperbolic eccentricity of about 3.36 was the first proof that interstellar objects exist at all, which 'Oumuamua's e of 1.20 had left in doubt", "Because a sample of one cannot distinguish typical from exceptional; the ordinary second object broke that degeneracy, confirming the prediction that most interstellar objects are comets and reclassifying 'Oumuamua as a genuine outlier", "Because its high CO content proved that all interstellar objects form around cool stars, resolving where 'Oumuamua itself had originated"], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "With a sample of one, weird and representative are indistinguishable. Borisov was a plain textbook comet, with a coma, a dust tail, and familiar molecules (cyanogen, then water) behaving exactly as an ordinary comet's should. That ordinariness broke the degeneracy: it confirmed the theoretical expectation that most interstellar objects should be comets, because comets, the volatile-rich planetesimals of a system's cold outskirts, are the easiest material for giant planets to scatter away. It thereby reclassified 'Oumuamua as a true outlier rather than a representative sample, a stronger statement than 'the first one was weird.' The high CO/H2O ratio is a legible thermometer pointing to a very cold, distant birthplace, but it is a quirk within the range, not a claim about every object; and Borisov's e of 3.36 was more extreme than 'Oumuamua's but not the first proof of the class, since 'Oumuamua's e above one already qualified."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "3I/ATLAS (2025) arrived at an excess velocity of about 58 km/s, roughly twice 'Oumuamua's. How does the chapter turn that speed into an estimate of the object's age?", "options": ["Faster objects have experienced more gravitational slingshots from planets, and counting those encounters over 58 km/s gives a direct age of about seven to eleven billion years", "Stellar orbits are dynamically heated over billions of years by encounters with molecular clouds and spiral arms, so high velocity relative to the local standard statistically marks an old, thick-disk star, implying an age of order seven to eleven billion years, plausibly older than the 4.6-billion-year-old Sun", "The high speed compressed and heated the object during its passage, and the degree of thermal alteration observed in its ices dates it to older than the Sun", "A larger excess velocity means a smaller, more tightly bound orbit, and only very old objects can shrink their semi-major axes to the values 3I/ATLAS showed"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The chain of reasoning uses kinematics as a clock. Stars are born from cold gas in the galactic plane moving with the calm, orderly, low-velocity flow of the disk, but over billions of years gravitational encounters with molecular clouds, spiral arms, and other stars heat their orbits, nudging them onto faster, plunging paths that lag the local rotation. So old stars, on average, move faster relative to the local standard of rest, and velocity is statistically age. The fast, puffed-up population is the thick disk, which is old, in places older than the Sun. An interstellar object carries the velocity of the star that made it, so 3I/ATLAS's high speed and approach direction place it in the thick disk, implying an age of order seven to eleven billion years, plausibly predating the 4.6-billion-year-old solar system. The velocity is not caused by planetary slingshots, thermal processing, or orbital shrinkage; it is inherited galactic kinematics."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "The chapter calls 'Oumuamua the case study in 'the discipline of the anomaly,' insisting that 'anomalous is not artificial.' Using Bayes' theorem, explain precisely why the fact that the lightsail hypothesis cleanly explains all three anomalies does NOT make those anomalies evidence for it. Identify what the lightsail argument establishes and the two things it neglects, and state the exact analogy the chapter draws to the ocean-worlds chapter.", "rubric": "A strong answer writes Bayes' theorem in the form P(artificial | data) is proportional to P(data | artificial) times P(artificial), and identifies that the lightsail argument establishes only the first factor, the likelihood: a thin, flat, light sail would indeed reproduce the extreme shape, the missing coma, and the radiation-pressure 1/r^2 acceleration, so P(data | artificial) is high. It should then name the two neglected factors. First, the prior P(artificial), the base rate that the very first interstellar object we ever detect happens to be manufactured hardware, which on any honest accounting is minuscule; extraordinary claims are extraordinary precisely because their base rate is low. Second, the competing likelihood P(data | natural), which is NOT small because the natural candidates (nitrogen ice, hydrogen ice, ultra-porous fractal aggregate) each reproduce the same data using materials or processes known to occur in nature. The key logical point: a high likelihood is necessary but not sufficient; the posterior is likelihood times prior normalised against the alternatives, so multiplying a near-one artificial likelihood by a vanishingly small prior, while a natural branch with a large prior also fits the data well, leaves the artificial posterior tiny. The rational conclusion is probably a natural object of a kind not yet catalogued, and the productive response is to find a second object rather than argue harder. For the analogy: the ocean-worlds chapter insisted that habitable is not inhabited, that a moon with the ingredients for life may not be assumed to host it; the exact analogue is that anomalous is not artificial, an object with the symptoms of strangeness may not be assumed to be technology. Both are the same refusal to infer a low-prior conclusion from data that a high-prior conclusion also explains, i.e. not letting the likelihood do the prior's job. A top answer may add, for fairness, that the lightsail proposal was not worthless: it forced the field to specify what a technological object would look like and exposed how thin the natural explanations were; the error was only in the last step, treating a hypothesis that could explain the data as though the data supported it before the mundane options were exhausted."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Explain the twofold scientific value of interstellar objects (what they are FOR) and why finding them has been so hard, then describe how the Vera C. Rubin Observatory (LSST) and ESA's Comet Interceptor together address that difficulty. Be specific about the number-density argument, the three detection obstacles, and the design logic of a spacecraft launched with no target.", "rubric": "A strong answer has three parts. (1) What they are for, two answers. First, individually, interstellar objects are the only material samples of other planetary systems we can examine: astronomy otherwise studies other stars and planets almost entirely by their light, but these bodies are actual condensed matter, ices and dust and rock assembled in another star's disk, brought into our neighbourhood where telescopes can dissect composition, watch ices sublimate in order (Borisov's CO, 3I/ATLAS's CO2 igniting before its water), and measure shape and spin, short of an interstellar probe we will not launch this century. Second, statistically, their number density measures how efficiently planetary systems eject material, a quantity essentially unmeasurable otherwise because ejecta are dark, cold, and scattered across parsecs. Every system is a cannon: growing giant planets scatter a large fraction of planetesimals clean out. The density implied by 'Oumuamua's single detection is of order 0.1 object per cubic AU, which, divided among the stars that made them, implies each star ejected something like an Earth mass or more of hundred-metre-and-larger bodies, and plausibly many Earth masses once the size distribution is included, meaning planet formation is prodigiously wasteful, exiling most solids to interstellar space. Borisov earns its keep again here: a galaxy seeded mostly with comets is consistent with ejection favouring easily-scattered volatile-rich outer-disk planetesimals. (2) The three detection obstacles: they are fast (in the inner system for weeks to a few months, then gone forever, at tens of km/s), faint (small, hundreds of metres to a couple of km, and dark and cold far from the Sun, near survey limits), and usually found on the way out (post-perihelion), the cruellest case because it maximises distance and minimises time left, foreclosing follow-up and any spacecraft response; 'Oumuamua was found about a month and a half after perihelion, already outbound, observable only about two weeks. Inbound discovery is far more valuable because it gives lead time to organise telescopes and, one day, a spacecraft. (3) The fixes: Rubin, an 8.4 m mirror with a 3,200-megapixel camera imaging the southern sky every few nights over the decade-long LSST, is a machine for catching faint fast-movers, expected to raise the discovery rate from a handful per decade to several or many per year, crucially many caught inbound, feeding an alert stream and target-of-opportunity apparatus. Comet Interceptor inverts the conventional find-then-build sequence, which cannot work because a target is discovered only weeks from perihelion, too late to build anything: instead build and launch first with no target, park at the Sun-Earth L2 point about 1.5 million km out, loiter for years holding station and fuel, then drop out and intercept when a survey finds a suitable inbound target (a pristine long-period comet or, the prize, an interstellar object), releasing sub-probes to image the nucleus. It is a mission built for an object that does not yet exist, the logical endpoint of wait-poised-until-the-galaxy-sends-something-worth-chasing."}]$astroISO_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/solar-system/small-bodies/interstellar-objects', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
